1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,128
Suppose the company that wrote heeyID closes its doors tonight.

2
00:00:04,178 --> 00:00:06,984
Its office is dark, and its servers are off.

3
00:00:07,034 --> 00:00:11,112
Tomorrow at eight, Aziz opens the mobile bank and logs in.

4
00:00:11,162 --> 00:00:14,984
Does it work?

5
00:00:15,034 --> 00:00:20,528
Lessons one and two followed Aziz on his journey, and gave the map and the words for it.

6
00:00:20,578 --> 00:00:23,360
This lesson shows where all of that runs.

7
00:00:23,410 --> 00:00:26,600
The answer is yes, and that answer is the whole design.

8
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:29,624
heeyID runs inside the perimeter of the bank.

9
00:00:29,674 --> 00:00:33,608
The bank installs it on its own servers, and the bank operates it.

10
00:00:33,658 --> 00:00:38,264
That copy is called an installation, and one installation serves one bank.

11
00:00:38,314 --> 00:00:42,056
A second bank gets a second installation, on its own servers.

12
00:00:42,106 --> 00:00:44,552
Nothing in the installation calls the vendor.

13
00:00:44,602 --> 00:00:48,584
There is no switch at the vendor that turns it off, and no data goes home.

14
00:00:48,634 --> 00:00:51,704
So the dark office changes nothing for Aziz.

15
00:00:51,754 --> 00:00:56,984
This is the most useful promise a vendor can give a bank: at night, the vendor is not needed.

16
00:00:57,034 --> 00:01:01,160
The personal data stays in the bank, in the Republic of Uzbekistan.

17
00:01:01,210 --> 00:01:03,512
Now look at the edge of the perimeter.

18
00:01:03,562 --> 00:01:07,232
Every call that goes out passes one gate, the egress proxy.

19
00:01:07,282 --> 00:01:11,936
The proxy lets out only the host names in the configuration of the installation.

20
00:01:11,986 --> 00:01:16,040
The list has three names, and all three are outside the bank.

21
00:01:16,090 --> 00:01:19,880
The first is MyID, which checks Aziz's face and passport.

22
00:01:19,930 --> 00:01:24,320
Only the MyID adapter calls it, with a credential from the key store of the bank.

23
00:01:24,370 --> 00:01:29,024
The second is the SMS vendor, which sends the text messages.

24
00:01:29,074 --> 00:01:35,240
It sends its delivery reports back through the edge, to one address of the SMS adapter.

25
00:01:35,290 --> 00:01:39,392
The third is the push service, which wakes up the app on Aziz's phone.

26
00:01:39,442 --> 00:01:44,528
Because it is outside, a push carries only the type of the message and a reference.

27
00:01:44,578 --> 00:01:48,176
The app then gets the text from heeyID, over its own session.

28
00:01:48,226 --> 00:01:50,984
TAF is missing from that list.

29
00:01:51,034 --> 00:01:57,488
Its agent already runs inside the bank network, and the TAF adapter calls only that agent.

30
00:01:57,538 --> 00:01:59,600
Nobody installs anything for it.

31
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:05,000
That is the whole outside world of heeyID: three names, through one gate.

32
00:02:05,050 --> 00:02:09,248
Inside the installation, there are three networks, one behind the other.

33
00:02:09,298 --> 00:02:12,464
First comes the edge, where all traffic enters.

34
00:02:12,514 --> 00:02:18,704
Then the application network, with the two gateways, Keycloak, the workers and the four adapters.

35
00:02:18,754 --> 00:02:23,288
Then the data network, with Postgres, Valkey, Kafka and Vault.

36
00:02:23,338 --> 00:02:25,904
The edge has no connection to the data network.

37
00:02:25,954 --> 00:02:29,120
A request must pass a gateway first.

38
00:02:29,170 --> 00:02:34,064
The bank also has two zones of its own: a test zone and a production zone.

39
00:02:34,114 --> 00:02:39,848
heeyID runs in both: stage in the test zone, and production in the production zone.

40
00:02:39,898 --> 00:02:44,744
In each zone, one Linux host runs the same units with Docker Compose.

41
00:02:44,794 --> 00:02:47,456
There is no network route between the two zones.

42
00:02:47,506 --> 00:02:51,920
Stage talks only to the MyID test service, with test identities.

43
00:02:51,970 --> 00:02:55,736
Production talks to the real MyID, with its own credential.

44
00:02:55,786 --> 00:03:01,928
Only configuration moves from stage to production, and a second person approves the move.

45
00:03:01,978 --> 00:03:04,064
No database content moves.

46
00:03:04,114 --> 00:03:05,936
And there is a guard at start-up.

47
00:03:05,986 --> 00:03:12,752
A production build refuses to start when its MyID host is a test host, or when test customers are allowed.

48
00:03:12,802 --> 00:03:15,032
Back to Aziz at eight in the morning.

49
00:03:15,082 --> 00:03:21,728
His login goes to the edge of his bank, then to the customer gateway, then to the records and the counters.

50
00:03:21,778 --> 00:03:25,136
His code comes through the TAF agent, inside the bank.

51
00:03:25,186 --> 00:03:29,432
Nothing on that path touches the vendor, so the dark office does not matter.

52
00:03:29,482 --> 00:03:37,240
Now a check: the push service is outside the bank, so what may a push to Aziz's phone carry?

53
00:03:37,290 --> 00:03:41,296
Only the type of the message and a reference, never his personal data.

54
00:03:41,346 --> 00:03:46,264
One installation for one bank, inside its perimeter, operated by the bank.

55
00:03:46,314 --> 00:03:48,136
No call to the vendor.

56
00:03:48,186 --> 00:03:55,024
Three names outside, through the egress proxy: MyID, the SMS vendor and the push service.

57
00:03:55,074 --> 00:03:57,808
Two zones, and the same units in each.

58
00:03:57,858 --> 00:04:04,072
Every installation starts from its configuration, and the MyID host is only one value in it.

59
00:04:04,122 --> 00:04:08,824
Lesson 4 shows that configuration, and why a missing value stops the start.
